<header class="ws-head"><p class="ws-kicker">Worksheet &middot; Cultures</p><h1 class="ws-title">Worksheet: Cultures of Sub-Saharan Africa</h1><p class="ws-lede">A worksheet on 5 cultures of Sub-Saharan Africa, from the San people to the Igbo-Ukwu culture. Read their profiles, then answer.</p><p class="ws-who" aria-hidden="true"><span>Name</span><span>Class</span><span>Date</span></p><p class="ws-tools"><button type="button" class="ws-print" data-key="0">Print the worksheet</button><button type="button" class="ws-print" data-key="1">Print with the answers</button><a href="/profiles?kind=culture&amp;region=Sub-Saharan%20Africa">Open the cultures &rarr;</a></p></header>
<section class="ws-task"><h2><span class="ws-n">1</span>Put the cultures in order</h2><p class="ws-how">Number the cultures from 1 (the earliest to begin) to 5 (the latest).</p><ul class="ws-order"><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>San people</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Nok culture</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Tuareg</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Khoekhoe</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Igbo-Ukwu culture</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="ws-task"><h2><span class="ws-n">2</span>Match the cultures to their homelands</h2><p class="ws-how">Write the number of the right homeland next to each culture. Their profiles will help.</p><div class="ws-match"><ol class="ws-ma" type="A"><li>San people</li><li>Nok culture</li><li>Khoekhoe</li><li>Tuareg</li><li>Igbo-Ukwu culture</li></ol><ol class="ws-mb"><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span>Southern Africa, from southern Zimbabwe, northern Botswana and the Limpopo south to the Cape</li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span>Southern Africa</li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span>The Sahara and Sahel from southwestern Libya and southern Algeria to Niger, Mali, Burkina Faso and northern…</li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span>Central Nigeria, in present-day southern Kaduna State and the neighbouring areas around the Jos Plateau</li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span>Igbo-Ukwu, a town in Anambra State, south-eastern Nigeria, on a ridge between the Aghomili and Obizi rivers</li></ol></div></section>
<section class="ws-task"><h2><span class="ws-n">3</span>Think and discuss</h2><ol class="ws-qs"><li><p><b>San people</b>. Its profile says: &#8220;The San, who call themselves by the names of their own nations such as the ǃKung, ǀXam and Khwe, descend from hunter-gatherer societies that some 10,000 years ago were spread from Tanzania to Cape Agulhas.&#8221; Find one piece of evidence on the profile for this, and say how we know it.</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><p><b>Nok culture</b>. Its profile says: &#8220;The Nok culture, named after the village in central Nigeria where its first sculpture was found in 1928, lasted from about 1500 BCE to around the turn of the Common Era.&#8221; Find one piece of evidence on the profile for this, and say how we know it.</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><p><b>Khoekhoe</b>. Its profile says: &#8220;By one account a proto-Khoe people emerged between 400 BCE and 100 CE in southern Zimbabwe, as San peoples met herding communities drifting south from East Africa; with their sheep and later cattle, Khoekhoe herders spread south to the Cape.&#8221; Find one piece of evidence on the profile for this, and say how we know it.</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><p>Choose two of these cultures that lived at the same time. What did they have in common, and what set them apart?</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li></ol></section>
<p class="ws-src">From The Epoch Institute: the-epoch-institute.org/profiles?kind=culture&amp;region=Sub-Saharan%20Africa. Free to print and copy for use in class.</p>
<details class="ws-key" id="answers"><summary>Answer key</summary><div class="ws-key-b"><p class="ws-key-t">Answer key: Worksheet: Cultures of Sub-Saharan Africa</p><section><h2><span class="ws-n">1</span>Put the cultures in order</h2><ol class="ws-key-list"><li>San people <span>c. 8000 BCE to today</span></li><li>Nok culture <span>c. 1500 BCE – c. 1 CE</span></li><li>Khoekhoe <span>c. 400 BCE to today</span></li><li>Tuareg <span>c. 400 CE to today</span></li><li>Igbo-Ukwu culture <span>c. 850 CE – c. 1050 CE</span></li></ol></section><section><h2><span class="ws-n">2</span>Match the cultures to their homelands</h2><ol class="ws-key-list" type="A"><li>San people <span>2: Southern Africa</span></li><li>Nok culture <span>4: Central Nigeria, in present-day southern Kaduna State and the neighbouring areas around the Jos Plateau</span></li><li>Khoekhoe <span>1: Southern Africa, from southern Zimbabwe, northern Botswana and the Limpopo south to the Cape</span></li><li>Tuareg <span>3: The Sahara and Sahel from southwestern Libya and southern Algeria to Niger, Mali, Burkina Faso and northern…</span></li><li>Igbo-Ukwu culture <span>5: Igbo-Ukwu, a town in Anambra State, south-eastern Nigeria, on a ridge between the Aghomili and Obizi rivers</span></li></ol></section><section><h2><span class="ws-n">3</span>Think and discuss</h2><p class="ws-key-note">Open questions: answers will vary. Good answers use details from the profiles.</p></section></div></details>
